# Task 1 — Fix the three live bugs

**Prompt:**

> In `scripts/audit/package-contract-upgrade-utils.js`, fix three defects. Do not change any function signatures.
>
> 1. `compareVersions` accepts malformed input. `Number("")` returns 0, not NaN, so `"1..0"` currently parses as `[1,0,0]` and compares equal to `"1.0.0"`. The loop is also hardcoded to `i < 3`, so `"1.2.3.4"` compares equal to `"1.2.3"`. Replace the parsing with a strict regex `^(0|[1-9]\d*)\.(0|[1-9]\d*)\.(0|[1-9]\d*)$` applied to the trimmed string, throwing on any non-match, and assert exactly three components.
> 2. `getConfirmations` branches on `hre.network.name`, which is a config label and does not prove which chain the RPC is connected to. Add an exported `assertChainId(expectedChainId)` that reads `(await ethers.provider.getNetwork()).chainId` and throws a message naming both values on mismatch. Keep `getConfirmations` but have it branch on the actual chainId (31337, 1337 → 1) rather than the name.
> 3. `getSigner` silently falls back to `ethers.getSigners()[0]`. Add an exported `assertExplicitSigner(signer, chainId)` that throws if `signer` is falsy and chainId is not 31337 or 1337.
>
> Write unit tests in `test/scripts/version-compare.test.js` covering: `"1..0"`, `"1.2.3.4"`, `"1.02.0"`, `"0x10.0.0"`, `""`, `null`, `"1.2"`, and the ordering cases `1.2.3 < 1.10.0` and `2.0.0 > 1.99.99`. Every malformed input must throw.

**Done when:** all malformed-input tests throw; ordering tests pass; `assertChainId` throws on a deliberate mismatch.

